WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.280
No. Mitch is fine.

00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:02.660
Still mad at me though.

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:05.760
You know how touchy he can be, Didi.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.030
Remember when we tried to cheer him up after

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:09.950
his first ice skating competition?

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:12.560
Yeah. How were we supposed to know?

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:14.330
Third place is a good thing.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.330
Memory tromped off across that muddy field

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:19.370
in his skates. He sunk like a woolly

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:22.350
mammoth. Hey, Didi, if you're around the 4th

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:23.780
of July, you should come by and watch the

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:27.320
wedding video. Oh, no.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:28.850
We're out of town in August.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:30.980
All right. Well, soon.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:34.190
Okay. Oh, Didi, I don't think I have your

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:34.610
new.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.380
Okay. It's magic time, everyone.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.780
Phil, please take your place beneath our

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:49.430
majestic arch.

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.550
Cue the quartet. Where's the other one?

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:54.350
He rode with the caterer who flipped his

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:54.890
truck.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.410
Why do good things happen to everyone but me?

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.330
Let's send the flower girl before she's a

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:00.500
flower teenager.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.650
Oh. So sad.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:05.310
Sorry.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:07.920
What is. Yeah.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.090
Like I'm the problem.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:10.650
Is your dad here?

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.680
Yeah. You should be right. Oh, my God, he's

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.060
out on the sidewalk on the phone.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:16.560
Do you want me to go get him?

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.900
No, no I don't. Come on, let's just do this.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:19.980
Oh.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:24.210
Mitchell, is this really how you want to get

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:24.570
married?

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.040
Well, it's not ideal now.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.600
People don't have any rice to throw because

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.520
they're chewing it raw out of starvation.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.320
Half of our guests are on the lawn, and

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:33.330
there go the sprinklers.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.610
Okay, let's just call time of death on this.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:36.840
All right.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:38.130
Hey, everybody.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.620
Um, thank you so much for, uh, for dealing

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.390
with all this today. It means a lot to us,

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.130
but unfortunately, this is not gonna.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:45.180
You can't do this. Yeah.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:46.440
I'm way ahead of you, Dad. And thank you for

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:47.010
stopping by.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:49.140
What I mean is, you can't get married like

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.480
this. You two deserve the kind of wedding

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.310
you've been talking about nonstop for the

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:54.330
last nine months.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:55.140
What do you mean?

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:56.430
You bring it up every chance that you get.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:57.960
I know what nonstop means.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.220
Trust me. Okay.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.070
Now what are we all standing around for?

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.260
My son's getting married today.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:08.170
All right. Jake. It's what this golf club

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:09.610
needs. Shake it up a little.

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:11.650
Go on dear. How often do your fathers get

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:12.370
married?

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:13.510
So far.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:15.610
One, two.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:17.020
Why am I still seeing you?

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:21.620
You take your seat Dad we're about to start.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:23.330
Actually, I thought you and I would take a

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:24.170
little walk.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.680
I remember it like it was yesterday, the day

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:32.960
that Mitchell came home from the hospital in

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.860
a very unfashionable white diaper with three

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:40.310
strands of scraggly, Raggedy Andy hair.

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:42.350
If he's doing it, I'm doing it.

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:43.520
You comin and Barb.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:45.740
You all sweet talker.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:47.390
Wait for me.

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:51.170
We were inseparable.

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:56.540
I was his big sister, his big brother, his

00:02:56.540 --> 00:03:00.380
nemesis, his protector, his best friend.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:06.140
I was his first partner, and I loved every

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:07.160
minute of it.

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:09.530
Thank you all for being here on this happy

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:13.130
occasion. They say the best marriages

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:15.770
contain just a little bit of magic.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:20.210
Believe me, I know.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.240
We're gathered here today to join two

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:26.260
amazing people.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:28.750
So naturally, I was a little picky when it

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:31.540
came to who was going to be replacing me.

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:33.340
And then I met this Cam.

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:37.630
And sure, he was warm and funny and loving.

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:43.000
Cameron, do you take Mitchell to be your

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.260
husband? I do.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.990
Cameron, would you please place this ring on

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:51.470
Mitchell's finger? Mitchell, do you take

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:53.060
Cameron to be your husband?

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:54.860
I do.

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:58.070
Then, by the powers vested in me by the state

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:00.380
of California, I am privileged to pronounce

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:02.030
you spouses for life.

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:04.250
You may now kiss your husband.

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:12.500
I wondered.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:15.260
Was he really everything I wanted for my

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:15.920
brother?

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:19.490
Was he really the best person?

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:22.880
No. I'm the best person.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:28.910
And now I am very pleased to raise a glass

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:31.310
and introduce, for the first time ever on

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:35.330
our fourth try, great husbands, Mitch and

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:36.020
Cam.

